gnubg-web: deterministic noise as intended (mean zero).

Noise() sums the 16 bytes of an MD5 hash and subtracts 2040, the mean of 16
bytes in 0..255. The bytes are stored as plain char, which is signed on most
targets (WebAssembly, x86, Apple arm64): the sum then averages about -8 and
every noisy evaluation is shifted by about -6.9 * rNoise (beginner: -0.42 on
the win probability). Checker play barely notices (all candidates get the same
shift), but cube decisions do: at beginner..intermediate gnubg beavered about
97% of doubles in double/take positions. Reading the bytes as unsigned char
gives the zero-mean noise the code comment and the manual describe.

--- a/eval.c
+++ b/eval.c
@@ -2267,7 +2267,11 @@
 
         r = 0.0f;
         for (i = 0; i < 16; i++)
-            r += auch[i];
+            /* gnubg-web: the bytes must be read as 0..255 (mean 2040, as the
+             * constants below assume). Plain char is signed on most targets,
+             * including WebAssembly, x86 and Apple arm64, which turned the
+             * noise into a bias of about -6.9 * rNoise on every output. */
+            r += (unsigned char) auch[i];
 
         r -= 2040.0f;
         r /= 295.6f;
